#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

void insertAndMaintainOrder(std::vector<int>& vec, int target, int value, bool maintainOrder) {
    auto it = std::find(vec.begin(), vec.end(), target);
    if (it == vec.end()) {
        vec.push_back(value);
    } else {
        if (maintainOrder && it != vec.begin()) {
            auto prevIt = it;
            --prevIt;
            if (*prevIt > *it) {
                std::iter_swap(prevIt, it);
            }
        }
        vec.insert(++it, value);
    }
}
```

This code uses the `std::find()` function to locate the first occurrence of `target` in the vector. If `target` is not found, `push_back()` is used to add `value` at the end of the vector. If `target` is found and `maintainOrder` is true, the `std::iter_swap()` function is used to ensure that the inserted element is in its correct position with respect to the surrounding elements. The vector is then efficiently updated using the `insert()` function to insert the new element after the location of `target`. By using iterators and in-place operations, this solution avoids unnecessary copying and memory allocation, which makes it suitable for handling large vectors.']

